# Target B: exact phase-deficit to Mantel-slack transfer

Date: 2026-10-03
Base main: 7a327e1ad95cd2e9cb035b9b96fb34fd8f12114d
Status: conditional exact algebra, noncanonical. Target B and Level I remain open.

The identities below apply to any finite family of retained phase-coarea grains
with the class sizes and crossing counts defined here, provided their vertex sets
are disjoint subsets of the global train set and their retained crossing counts
sum to `R_*`. For the displayed Mantel comparisons, assume each grain satisfies
`Delta_i >= 0`; canonical Section 51.1 supplies this when the grain is one of its
retained phase-core components, while other grain models must establish it
separately.

Any positive lower bound `Delta_* >= delta`, high-mass condition on `Theta`,
coarea decomposition, or asymptotic relation between `R_*` and `D^2` is an
additional input. This note does not prove those inputs or promote any separate
phase-deficit candidate. The algebraic implications do not use a verifier or
numerical search.

## 1. Exact one-grain identity

Let a retained phase-coarea grain have coherent phase-class sizes

    M >= b >= a,

and write

    s=a/M,   t=b/M,   u=s+t,

    Delta=[M(a+b)-E]/M^2,

where E is its retained physical crossing count. Thus

    E=M^2(u-Delta).                                      (1)

The grain uses exactly

    V=M+a+b=M(1+u)                                      (2)

train vertices.

### Lemma 1 — phase deficit is Mantel slack

One has the exact identity

    V^2/4-E
      = M^2 [ Delta + (u-1)^2/4 ].                       (3)

Equivalently,

    V^2-4(1+Delta)E
      = M^2 [u-(1+2Delta)]^2.                            (4)

Proof. Expanding (1)-(2),

    V^2/4-E
      = M^2[(1+u)^2/4-u+Delta]
      = M^2[Delta+(u-1)^2/4].

Subtracting Delta E from both sides of (3), or expanding directly, gives

    V^2/4-(1+Delta)E
      = M^2[u-(1+2Delta)]^2/4.

This is (4). QED.

Two consequences are immediate.

First,

    boxed{ V >= 2 sqrt((1+Delta)E). }                    (5)

This strictly strengthens componentwise Mantel whenever Delta>0. Equality in
(5) is possible only when

    u=1+2Delta.                                          (6)

Second, if E>0 and

    M_H:=V^2/4-E

is the ordinary graph-theoretic Mantel slack of the grain, then

    boxed{ M_H/E >= Delta. }                             (7)

So the Theorem-51 phase deficit is a direct lower bound for relative Mantel
slack.

## 2. Global coarea transfer

Let the retained coarea grains be H_i, with crossing counts E_i, train counts
V_i, and phase deficits Delta_i. The grain vertex sets are disjoint subsets of
the global train set; trains isolated after the coarea cut only increase T.
Hence

    T >= sum_i V_i.                                      (8)

Also

    R_*=sum_i E_i.                                       (9)

Applying (5) grainwise gives

    boxed{
    T >= 2 sum_i sqrt((1+Delta_i)E_i).
    }                                                    (10)

This is the general-phase analogue of the balanced-grain train budget, with no
equal-phase-size assumption.

Now distinguish one grain H_* and put

    Theta=E_*/R_*.

Assume `R_*>0` for this normalization.

If Delta_* >= delta, then all other grains still satisfy ordinary Mantel, so

    T
      >= 2 sqrt((1+delta)E_*)
         +2 sum_{i != *} sqrt(E_i)

      >= 2 sqrt(R_*)[
             sqrt((1+delta)Theta)+sqrt(1-Theta)
           ].                                            (11)

The last step uses

    sum_{i != *} sqrt(E_i)
      >= sqrt(sum_{i != *} E_i).

If, separately, the retained grains satisfy

    R_*/D^2 -> Delta_2=pi/(2sqrt(3)),

then every subsequence on which Theta -> theta and liminf Delta_* >= delta satisfies

    boxed{
    liminf T/D
      >= 2 sqrt(Delta_2)
         [sqrt((1+delta)theta)+sqrt(1-theta)].
    }                                                    (12)

Under the stated grain and mass premises, this is a direct local-to-global
conversion of an assumed GPI phase gap into train population.

## 3. Deficit-refined grain concentration

Equation (11) can also be inverted. Put

    A=1+delta,
    L=X/[2 sqrt(Delta_2)].

Assume the same retained-mass premise as in (12),
`R_*/D^2 -> Delta_2`.

Assume the largest grain obeys Delta_* >= delta and a subsequence satisfies

    T/D <= X+o(1).

Assume also the high-mass branch condition

    Theta >= A/(A+1),

and the admissible range

    sqrt(A) <= L <= sqrt(A+1).

On this branch the function

    f_delta(Theta)=sqrt(A Theta)+sqrt(1-Theta)

is strictly decreasing. Hence (11) implies

    Theta >= C_delta(X)-o(1),                            (13)

where the high-mass root is

    C_delta(X)
      = 1 - [
          {L-sqrt(A(A+1-L^2))over A+1}
        ]^2.                                             (14)

For delta=0 this reduces to the usual two-grain Mantel concentration bound.
For every fixed delta>0 in the same admissible range,

    C_delta(X)>C_0(X).                                   (15)

Thus a positive GPI deficit gives a strict strengthening of the two-grain
concentration bound whenever its high-mass and admissibility conditions hold.
Those conditions and a usable `delta` require a separate theorem. The current
route's canonical GPI gives a local deficit only for a dominant coherent
component with `t=b/M >= tau > 0`; applying it here requires verifying that exact
component and parameter scope. This note does not derive a 79% mass concentration
or a fixed `delta` from `T/D <= 2.56`.

## 4. Exact equality conditions

For exact equality in (11), first there can be no trains outside the retained
grains, and the distinguished-grain replacement requires `Delta_*=delta` when
`E_*>0`. Equality in the grain terms and residual sum then requires the following.

For equality in the distinguished-grain term, (6) requires

    s_*+t_*=1+2Delta_*.

For every residual grain contributing positive crossing mass, equality in its
ordinary Mantel bound requires

    Delta_i=0,
    s_i+t_i=1.

Finally, equality in

    sum_{i != *} sqrt(E_i)
      >= sqrt(sum_{i != *}E_i)

requires at most one residual grain to carry positive crossing mass.

Thus exact equality in (11) requires at most two crossing-carrying grains: the
distinguished grain lies on `u=1+2Delta`, and any residual crossing-carrying
grain lies on the `Delta=0, u=1` Mantel locus. These are equality conditions
only; no quantitative near-equality stability theorem is proved here.

## 5. What this resolves and what remains

Equations (5), (10), and (11) convert an assumed phase deficit into a train
population lower bound at fixed retained crossing mass. The report does not prove
that the grains partition all leading crossing mass, that one grain has positive
`Delta`, or that the high-mass condition holds. The canonical local GPI does not
by itself provide this multi-grain globalization. Target B remains open; any use
of this transfer must first establish its inputs.

The remaining global task is to:

1. strengthen the local GPI deficit enough that (11) exceeds the Section-39
   allowance; or
2. combine the equality structure in Section 4 with the physical shell,
   penetration, or boundary ledger to show that the two-grain near-equality
   profile has an additional positive cost.

No computational evidence is used as a proof premise.
